1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:14,300
Every austral summer, India's research stations are resupplied through Antarctic sea ice — where the ice charts are days

2
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:21,496
old, icebergs move, and the ice edge can shift overnight. POLARIS-X turns open satellite and model data into a

3
00:00:21,546 --> 00:00:28,430
risk picture, route options, and a re-planning decision — and it keeps the human in command at every step.

4
00:00:30,990 --> 00:00:37,974
This is the running system, in LIVE mode. Everything on screen is computed from real datasets — NSIDC sea-ice

5
00:00:38,024 --> 00:00:46,556
concentration, USNIC iceberg positions, BYU and NIC iceberg tracks, and Open-Meteo wind and temperature. Every layer declares what it

6
00:00:46,606 --> 00:00:53,138
is and how old it is: observation, model output, or planned. Ocean currents and waves are planned — no

7
00:00:53,188 --> 00:00:59,720
data source is connected yet, so the system says so, instead of drawing something that does not exist.

8
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:10,827
This is the latest NSIDC daily analysis in our data window — the first of September, mean concentration thirty-nine point eight

9
00:01:10,877 --> 00:01:19,000
percent along the corridor. I can step back through eight observation days, or forecast forward. At plus forty-eight hours the damped-trend

10
00:01:19,050 --> 00:01:26,174
model gives forty-two percent, with an uncertainty band of five point seven percent and a backtest error of four percent —

11
00:01:26,224 --> 00:01:33,700
against four point two for persistence. The badge changes to SEA-ICE FORECAST, because this is model output, not an observation.

12
00:01:38,470 --> 00:01:45,823
Thirteen icebergs are charted in this area; seventeen have real tracks from the BYU database, and five are classified as

13
00:01:45,873 --> 00:01:53,534
moving. The drift forecast carries an empirical P90 corridor of about forty-two kilometres at seven days. And the card states

14
00:01:53,584 --> 00:01:59,950
openly that the track archive lags real time — which is why current positions come from the USNIC chart.

15
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:15,134
Risk is the core product: the IMO POLARIS risk index for sea ice, Overland's icing predictor, and iceberg

16
00:02:15,184 --> 00:02:22,262
hazard zones — combined worst-case, never averaged. With a PC5 ice class, the worst cell in the area

17
00:02:22,312 --> 00:02:28,891
is normal operation: RIO plus ten. Now watch the only thing I change — the vessel. PC7: minus

18
00:02:28,941 --> 00:02:35,163
nine, and a quarter of the cells move into the elevated band. No ice class at all: minus

19
00:02:35,213 --> 00:02:42,790
forty-nine, and over half the area becomes critical. Same ocean, same data — the vessel changes the answer.

20
00:02:54,720 --> 00:03:01,680
For routing, the optimizer does not invent cost weights — it applies severity ceilings as hard constraints.

21
00:03:01,730 --> 00:03:08,821
DIRECT is eight hundred and sixty-seven nautical miles, sixty-nine hours, with twenty percent of the route in

22
00:03:08,871 --> 00:03:15,438
medium severity. CONSERVATIVE costs a hundred and thirty extra miles to keep the whole route low. The

23
00:03:15,488 --> 00:03:23,169
recommendation cites IMO circular 1519 — avoid elevated-risk areas when planning. And fuel reads NOT COMPUTED, because

24
00:03:23,219 --> 00:03:28,410
no validated consumption model exists. We do not guess numbers we cannot defend.

25
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:51,148
Now the real workflow — a resupply mission. Origin picked on the map, destination Bharati station in Prydz Bay, departure

26
00:03:51,198 --> 00:03:58,321
the second of September. The wizard validates the date against the actual data window — the twenty-fifth of August to

27
00:03:58,371 --> 00:04:04,881
the fourth of September — and tells me which observation and which weather hour it will use. Ice class PC5,

28
00:04:04,931 --> 00:04:11,380
twelve and a half knots, and a deliberately strict ceiling: nothing worse than low on the remaining route.

29
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:23,444
The departure date is validated against the real data window before the mission is accepted — no silent substitution. The optimizer then runs on

30
00:04:23,494 --> 00:04:31,890
the risk surface resolved for that departure time: one profile per severity ceiling, each one either a feasible plan or an honest refusal.

31
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:45,197
Three options for that departure — all eight hundred and sixty-nine miles and low at this hour. I take the recommended DIRECT line.

32
00:04:45,247 --> 00:04:52,354
Acceptance is a human click: the system never starts a voyage by itself. The vessel then moves along the accepted geometry at the

33
00:04:52,404 --> 00:04:59,732
optimizer's own speed model — there is no decorative animation. I advance the mission clock twelve hours at a time; conditions at the

34
00:04:59,782 --> 00:05:07,110
vessel update with the clock, and every three simulated hours the remaining route is re-assessed against the data valid at that time.

35
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:17,594
There it is — ROUTE REVIEW REQUIRED. The re-assessment found medium severity on the remaining route, which breaks the ceiling the operator set.

36
00:05:17,644 --> 00:05:24,993
It names the primary factor, and it pauses. It does not reroute on its own. I ask for alternatives from our current position,

37
00:05:25,043 --> 00:05:33,399
at the current time. Three candidates; CONSERVATIVE is recommended — five hundred and seventy miles, and the smallest exposure of the three. I

38
00:05:33,449 --> 00:05:40,620
accept it: the active route changes, the track restarts from this point, and the decision is written into the mission log.

39
00:05:54,400 --> 00:06:01,845
For the finale, a repeatable drill. It simulates exactly one fact — iceberg D23 ungrounding and drifting a hundred

40
00:06:01,895 --> 00:06:09,274
kilometres north, into our corridor — and every route, every risk number and every corridor radius is computed by

41
00:06:09,324 --> 00:06:16,769
the same live engines you have just seen. Twenty-four hours at cruise speed: three hundred miles covered, then the

42
00:06:16,819 --> 00:06:24,264
simulated re-sighting arrives, and D23 is inside the corridor. The remaining leg goes from low to critical, with a

43
00:06:24,314 --> 00:06:32,679
closest approach of two and a half kilometres. Re-planning recommends CONSERVATIVE: five hundred and ninety-two miles, all low —

44
00:06:32,729 --> 00:06:38,530
twenty-five miles longer than staying put. I accept it, and the new route becomes active.

45
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:46,561
POLARIS-X predicts, assesses and recommends — from real open data, with every

46
00:06:46,611 --> 00:06:51,370
product labelled by its source. The officer on the bridge decides.
